      "claim": "Salicylic acid, an aromatic carboxylic acid with a nearby polar group, has oral bioavailability of 0.92 relative to IV, strongly supporting high oral absorption for the query.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C21"],
      "claim": "4-Phenylbutyric acid, a lipophilic carboxylic acid, has absolute oral bioavailability of 78% in humans, supporting high F for the query's carboxylic acid and aromatic ketone structure.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C07"],
      "claim": "Setipiprant, a larger lipophilic carboxylic acid, has human oral bioavailability of at least 50%, supporting high F for the query despite increased molecular size.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C13"],
      "claim": "Ampicillin, a carboxylic acid-containing drug with more challenging absorption characteristics, still shows 30-50% oral bioavailability in humans, above the 20% threshold.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    }
  ],
  "confidence": "high",
  "decision_summary": "All highly transferable experimental analogues are carboxylic acids with measured absolute oral bioavailability consistently above 20%, mostly 30-100%. The query's carboxylic acid and moderate lipophilicity align with these high-absorption phenotypes, so F ≥ 20% is strongly supported.",
  "evidence_gaps": [
    "No direct experimental bioavailability record for the query molecule itself.",
    "No contradictory evidence at or below the 20% threshold was retrieved.",
    "Some analogues are zwitterionic or beta-lactam antibiotics with transporter-mediated absorption, but their measured F values still exceed 20%."
  ],
